[{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Moment-calculations","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The integration assumes each angular bin has the same velocity direction hatmathbf e(Omega) at every energy. Spacecraft potential changes the particle speed while preserving this direction, giving mathbf v_infty = sqrt2E_inftymhatmathbf e(Omega).\n\nSince E_infty = E + qPhi_sc, we have dE_infty = dE.\n\nd^3v_infty = sqrtfrac2E_inftym^3 dE_infty dOmega_infty = sqrtfrac2E_inftym^3 dE dOmega","title":"Moment calculations"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Angular-weights","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"Each angle bin i is the patch theta_i pm Deltatheta_i2, phi_i pm Deltaphi_i2, on which f_E is taken constant. The angular factors in the sums below are exact integrals over the patch:\n\nDeltaOmega_i = int_i dOmega qquad\nOmega_ji = int_i hat e_j dOmega qquad\nOmega_jki = int_i hat e_j hat e_k dOmega","title":"Angular weights"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Number-density","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The ambient number density is given by\n\nn = int f(mathbf v_) d^3v_infty\n\nwhere f(mathbf v_) is the ambient phase-space distribution function.\n\nThe differential number flux, which is measured by particle instruments, is related to the velocity distribution by\n\nj(EOmega) dE dOmega = v f(mathbf v_) d^3v \n\nWhere the measured energy E is related to the measured speed v by E=m v^2  2. We get\n\nj(EOmega) = frac2Em^2 f(mathbf v_)\n\nThe differential energy flux therefore satisfies\n\nf_E(EOmega) = E j(EOmega) = frac2E^2m^2 f(mathbf v_)\n\nso f(mathbf v_infty) = fracm^22E^2 f_E. Substituting the relation between f and f_E into n yields\n\nn = sqrtfracm2 int f_E(EOmega) fracsqrtE_E^2 dE dOmega\n\nAnd after discretization in energy and angle,\n\nn = sqrtfracm2 sum_i f_Ei a_i fracΔ E_iE_i ^ 2 sqrtE_i  Δ Omega_i\n\nwhere the weight a_i suppresses bins below the spacecraft potential, which hold photoelectrons:\n\na_i = mathrmclampleft(fracE_i + qPhi_sc Δ E_i + frac12 0 1right)","title":"Number density"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Flux","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The particle flux is the first velocity-space moment (vector):\n\nmathbf F = int mathbf v_infty f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\nqquad\nF_j = int v_inftyj f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\n\nWe obtain\n\nF_j = int left(sqrtfrac2E_inftym hat e_jright)\nleft(sqrtfrac2E_inftym^3right)\nfracm^22E^2 f_E  dE dOmega\n= int f_E  fracE_inftyE^2 hat e_j dE dOmega\n\nAfter discretization in energy and angle, this becomes\n\nF_j = sum_i f_Ei a_i fracDelta E_iE_i ^2 E_inftyi Omega_ji\nqquad j in xyz\n\nCompared to density, the integrand acquires one extra factor of E_inftyE, and Omega_ji replaces DeltaOmega_i.","title":"Flux"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Energy-flux","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The energy flux is the energy-weighted first moment:\n\nmathbf F_E = int E_infty mathbf v_infty f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\nqquad\nF_Ej = int E_infty v_inftyj f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\n\nUsing the same substitutions as above yields\n\nF_Ej = int f_E fracE_infty^2E^2 hat e_j dE dOmega\n\nAfter discretization in energy and angle,\n\nF_Ej = sum_i f_Ei a_i fracDelta E_iE_i^2 E_inftyi^2 Omega_ji\nqquad j in xyz","title":"Energy flux"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Momentum-flux","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The (symmetric) momentum flux tensor is the second velocity-space moment (rank-2 tensor):\n\nmathrmMF_jk = int m v_inftyj v_inftyk f(mathbf v_infty) d^3 v_infty\n\nUsing the relation between (1) f and f_E, (2) d^3v_infty and dE dOmega, we obtain\n\nmathrmMF_jk\n= int m left(sqrtfrac2E_inftymhat e_jright)\nleft(sqrtfrac2E_inftymhat e_kright)\nleft(sqrtfrac2E_inftym^3right)\nfracm^22E^2 f_E  dE dOmega\n= sqrt2 m int f_E  fracE_infty^32E^2 hat e_jhat e_k dE dOmega\n\nAfter discretization in energy and angle, this becomes\n\nmathrmMF_jk = sqrt2 msum_i\nf_Ei a_i fracDelta E_iE_i^2 E_inftyi^32 Omega_jki\nqquad (jk) in xyz^2\n\nIn code and many data products, the symmetric tensor is stored as (xx yy zz xy xz yz).","title":"Momentum flux"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Pressure-tensor","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The pressure tensor is the second central moment (momentum flux minus the bulk-flow/ram contribution):\n\nP_jk = mathrmMF_jk - V_jF_k m","title":"Pressure tensor"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_note/#Heat-flux","page":"Math notes for moment calculations","text":"The heat flux is defined as the third velocity-space moment of the thermal (bulk-frame) velocity mathbf w_infty equiv mathbf v_infty - mathbf V:\n\nmathbf q = int tfrac12 mmathbf v_infty - mathbf V^2(mathbf v_infty - mathbf V) f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\nqquad\nq_j = int tfrac12 mmathbf v_infty - mathbf V^2(v_inftyj - V_j) f(mathbf v_infty) d^3v_infty\n\nHere mathbf V = mathbf Fn is the bulk velocity. Expanding mathbf w^2mathbf w with mathbf w = mathbf v_infty - mathbf V and integrating term by term expresses mathbf q through the lab-frame moments above:\n\nmathbf q = mathbf F_E - mathrmMFmathbf V - tfrac12operatornametr(mathrmMF)mathbf V + mnmathbf V^2mathbf V\n\nusing mathbf F_E = int tfrac12 m mathbf v_infty^2 mathbf v_infty f d^3v_infty and mathbf F = nmathbf V. Each term is integrated exactly over the bins, so mathbf q is too. SPEDAS instead evaluates mathbf w^2mathbf w at bin centers, which is off by O(Deltatheta^2 Deltaphi^2) and fails for wide bins such as a full-circle phi bin.","title":"Heat flux"},{"category":"section","location":"moment_validation/#Plasma-moments-from-MMS-ion-scans","page":"Moment validation","text":"This example computes ion moments from 66 MMS1 FPI burst scans and checks them against PySPEDAS. The fixture holds the raw distribution, the magnetic field and spacecraft potential at the scan times, and the PySPEDAS moments (generated by ref/generate_pyspedas_moments.jl).\n\nusing VelocityDistributionFunctions\nusing Downloads, JLD2\n\nurl = \"https://github.com/JuliaSpacePhysics/VelocityDistributionFunctions.jl/releases/download/v0.2.1/pyspedas_mms_fpi_brst_i_v2.jld2\"\nsaved = JLD2.load(Downloads.download(url), \"result\")\ndist = saved[\"dis_dist_brst\"]\nsize(dist[\"data\"])  # (time, φ, θ, energy), phase-space density in s³/cm⁶\n(66, 32, 16, 32)\n\nTwo FPI properties shape the grid:\n\nFPI angles are instrument look directions, but the grid takes particle velocity directions, so both angles are flipped.\nThe azimuth and energy tables change from scan to scan, so the grid is built once per scan.\n\nlayout names the axes of a single scan. tdim gives the time axis of the full array.\n\nphi = mod.(dist[\"phi\"] .+ 180, 360)\ntheta = 180 .- dist[\"theta\"]\ngrid = EnergyAngleGrid.(eachrow(dist[\"energy\"]), eachrow(phi), Ref(theta); layout = (:phi, :theta, :energy))\nmoments = plasma_moments(dist[\"data\"], grid; species = :H, units = :df_cm, tdim = 1,\n    sc_pot = saved[\"scpot_data\"], B = eachrow(saved[\"mag_data\"]))\n\nTemperatures agree with PySPEDAS to rtol = 1e-6. Density, velocity and fluxes differ by about 0.1% because PySPEDAS uses an approximate proton mass, here the CODATA value. The heat flux differs by up to 2% because PySPEDAS evaluates it at bin centers (Heat flux).\n\nref = saved[\"ref\"]\n@assert isapprox(moments.density, ref[\"density\"]; rtol = 2e-3)\n@assert isapprox(moments.avgtemp, ref[\"avgtemp\"]; rtol = 1e-6)\n(; density = moments.density[1:3], density_pyspedas = ref[\"density\"][1:3], avgtemp_eV = moments.avgtemp[1:3])\n(density = [12.092629471815197, 12.402743720180943, 11.871165411051694], density_pyspedas = [12.105850981450768, 12.41630429408141, 11.88414463864743], avgtemp_eV = [362.2606882585714, 353.0299636107013, 356.5343806940593])","title":"Plasma moments from MMS ion scans"},{"category":"section","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for VelocityDistributionFunctions.\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions"},{"category":"type","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.EnergyAngleGrid","page":"Home","text":"EnergyAngleGrid(energy, phi, theta; layout, denergy, dphi, dtheta)\n\nBin centers and widths of a spherical velocity-space grid: energy [eV] and the velocity direction phi, theta [deg].\n\nlayout names the data axes, from :energy, :phi, :theta and :angle (flattened direction), e.g. (:phi, :theta, :energy). A vector lies along its named axis; other arrays broadcast against the data. Missing widths are estimated along each coordinate's own axis, so a flattened :angle axis needs them supplied. Estimated energy widths are midpoints between centers, which overestimate the end bins of a log grid (by 10% for a 20% step); prefer the instrument's denergy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.EnergyAngleGrid"},{"category":"method","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.angle_distribution-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"angle_distribution(data, grid, coord; B = nothing, gyro_ref = nothing, bins = 12, method = :mean, tdim = nothing) -> (; data, solid_angle, edges, centers)\n\nFor :gyrophase, the projection of gyro_ref perpendicular to B defines 0°. Positive angles turn toward B × gyro_ref. Each cell goes whole to the bin of its center direction. Non-finite cells are skipped; bins without finite cells are NaN, as are all bins when B or gyro_ref defines no direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.angle_distribution"},{"category":"method","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.directional_spectra-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"directional_spectra(S, pitch_angles, loss_cone; half_sector_width = nothing, para_tol = 0, perp_tol = 0, method = :mean, tdim = nothing)\n\nWith l = min(loss_cone, 180° - loss_cone), sectors at pitch angle θ are para if θ < l - para_tol, anti if θ > 180° - l + para_tol, perp if l + perp_tol < θ < 180° - l - perp_tol; omni takes all. Sectors with non-finite flux or pitch angle are skipped; a direction left without sectors gives NaN.\n\n:mean weights sectors by solid angle 2Δθ sin θ, or Δθ sin Δθ within Δθ = half_sector_width of 0° or 180°. :sum does not compensate for skipped sectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.directional_spectra"},{"category":"method","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.group_mean-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"group_mean(data, groups; dims, weights = nothing)\n\nMean of data over each group of indices in groups (e.g. [1:3, 4:6]) along axis dims, weighted by weights (one per index along dims) if given. The output has the axes of data with one entry per group along dims. Non-finite values are skipped; a group left without values gives NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.group_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.plasma_moments-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"plasma_moments(data, grid; species, units, sc_pot = 0, B = nothing, tdim = nothing)\n\nMoments of one scan as a NamedTuple. With tdim, return a StructArray with one record per scan along that axis. Use one grid and spacecraft potential for all scans or one of each per scan. Supply series fields as one B vector per scan. Align ancillary measurements to the scan intervals before calling.\n\ndata is in units: :eflux [eV/(cm²·s·sr·eV)], :flux [1/(cm²·s·sr·eV)], :df_cm [s³/cm⁶], :df_km [s³/km⁶] or :df_m [s³/m⁶].\nspecies is :H, :He (α), :O (¹⁶O⁺), :e, or a PlasmaPopulations.Particle.\nsc_pot [V] shifts energies by charge * sc_pot.\nNon-finite data bins contribute zero; a scan without finite bins gives NaN moments.\nOutput NamedTuple is CGS with energies in eV: density [cm⁻³], velocity, vthermal [cm/s], flux [cm⁻² s⁻¹], eflux, qflux [eV cm⁻² s⁻¹], mftens, ptens [eV cm⁻³] as (xx, yy, zz, xy, xz, yz), ttens, avgtemp, t3 [eV].\nWith field B, adds magt3 [eV] (⊥, ⊥, ∥) and the symmetry axis symm with symm_theta, symm_phi, symm_ang [deg].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.plasma_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"#VelocityDistributionFunctions.sort_flux_by_pitch_angle!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"sort_flux_by_pitch_angle!(flux, pitch_angle; sort_on_reverse = true)\n\nSort flux by pitch angle into ascending order. Set sort_on_reverse to true to sort only if the pitch angle is in reverse order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VelocityDistributionFunctions.sort_flux_by_pitch_angle!"}]
